\description{
\code{blrm_markov_ml()} fits the Bayesian transition model of \code{\link[=blrm_markov]{blrm_markov()}},
with the same priors, when some outcome states are missing or known only up
to a range of states. Like \code{\link[=orm_markov_ml]{orm_markov_ml()}}, it uses every visit of every
patient: for a visit whose state is unknown, it adds up the probabilities of
all states the patient could have been in, instead of dropping the
transitions into and out of that visit. The posterior is sampled with Stan.
The result works with \code{\link[=sops]{sops()}}, \code{\link[=avg_sops]{avg_sops()}}, \code{\link[=avg_time]{avg_time()}}, and
\code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}} like a \code{\link[=blrm_markov]{blrm_markov()}} fit: these functions compute
their results for each posterior draw and summarise the draws. The model
can include partial proportional odds and a random intercept per patient.
}
\section{The model and the likelihood}{

The transition model is the one described for \code{\link[=orm_markov_ml]{orm_markov_ml()}}. For a visit
\eqn{t \ge 1} and a non-absorbing previous state \eqn{a},
\deqn{P(Y_{it} \ge j \mid Y_{i,t-1} = a) =
  \mathrm{logit}^{-1}(\alpha_j + x_{it}(a)^\top \beta), \quad j = 2, \dots, K,}
where \eqn{x_{it}(a)} is the design row built by \code{formula} from the
covariates on that row with \code{p_var} set to \eqn{a}, and absorbing states
are never left. With partial proportional odds (\code{ppo} and \code{cppo}), the
linear predictor for \eqn{Y \ge j} also contains \eqn{c(j)\, z_{it}(a)^\top
\tau}, where \eqn{z_{it}(a)} is the design row built by \code{ppo} and
\eqn{c(j)} is \code{cppo} evaluated at state \eqn{j}: the effects of the \code{ppo}
terms change across the states in proportion to \eqn{c(j)}.

Patient \eqn{i} contributes \eqn{L_i}, the probability of everything
observed for that patient given the starting state and covariates; at a
visit with a missing or range-coded state, \eqn{L_i} sums over the states
allowed there. The posterior density of the coefficients is proportional to
the prior density times \eqn{\prod_i L_i}. When no state is missing,
\eqn{\prod_i L_i} is the ordinary likelihood that \code{\link[=blrm_markov]{blrm_markov()}} uses, and
both functions sample the same posterior.

With \code{cluster(id)} in \code{formula} (using the patient ID column named by
\code{id_var}), each patient also has a random intercept \eqn{\gamma_i}, added
to the linear predictor of every transition of that patient. It describes
patients who tend to be in better or worse states than their covariates
and previous state predict. Given \eqn{\gamma_i}, the patient's
transitions still depend on the past only through the previous state, so
\eqn{L_i} is computed in the same way with \eqn{\gamma_i} added.

With partial proportional odds, some coefficient values make
\eqn{P(Y \ge j)} increase with \eqn{j}, which gives a negative probability
to a state. As in \code{\link[rmsb:blrm]{rmsb::blrm()}}, such values are excluded when the
affected state is observed. Here they are also excluded whenever they occur
for a visit the likelihood sums over, because that sum uses the
probabilities of all states.
}

\section{Priors}{

The priors are those of \code{\link[rmsb:blrm]{rmsb::blrm()}}:
\itemize{
\item Intercepts: a Dirichlet prior with concentration \code{conc} on the state
probabilities of a patient whose covariates (including the previous
state) equal their averages over the transitions with both states
observed. Smaller values of \code{conc} make the prior less informative.
\item Regression coefficients: flat (improper) priors, except for contrasts
that \code{pcontrast} (for \code{formula} terms) or \code{npcontrast} (for \code{ppo} terms)
give normal priors. Both are specified as in \code{\link[rmsb:blrm]{rmsb::blrm()}}. For example,
\code{pcontrast = list(sd = 1, c1 = list(tx = 1), c2 = list(tx = 0), contrast = expression(c1 - c2))} gives the log odds ratio between
\code{tx = 1} and \code{tx = 0} a normal prior with mean 0 and standard deviation
\enumerate{
\item Variables not named in the settings are held at the values set by
\code{\link[rms:datadist]{rms::datadist()}}, so set \code{options(datadist = )} before fitting when the
model has other covariates.
}
\item Random intercepts: normal with mean 0 and standard deviation
\eqn{\sigma}. With \code{psigma = 1} (the default), \eqn{\sigma} has a
half-\eqn{t} prior with 4 degrees of freedom, location \code{rsdmean}, and
scale \code{rsdsd}; with \code{psigma = 2}, an exponential prior with mean
\code{rsdmean}. These are the \code{\link[rmsb:blrm]{rmsb::blrm()}} priors.
}
}

\section{Random effects in predictions}{

\code{\link[=sops]{sops()}}, \code{\link[=avg_sops]{avg_sops()}}, \code{\link[=avg_time]{avg_time()}}, and \code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}} leave the
random intercepts out by default (\code{include_re = FALSE}): predictions are
then for a patient whose random intercept is 0, a patient typical of those
with the same covariates. With \code{include_re = TRUE}, each patient's
predictions use that patient's posterior draws of \eqn{\gamma_i}, so
averages over the stored patients describe these patients, including how
they differ beyond their covariates. \code{include_re = TRUE} needs prediction
data whose patient IDs are those of the fit, such as the stored starting
profiles used when \code{newdata} is omitted.
}

\section{Sampling and diagnostics}{

The function compiles a Stan program with \pkg{rstan} the first time it is
used. Compiling needs a C++ toolchain (Rtools on Windows) and takes a few
minutes. The compiled program is saved in
\code{tools::R_user_dir("mostr", "cache")} and reused in later R sessions. Set
\code{options(mostr.stan_cache_dir = )} to another directory to store it
elsewhere, or to \code{FALSE} to keep it only for the current session.

Each of the \code{chains} Markov chains runs \code{iter} iterations. The first
\code{warmup} iterations tune the sampler and are discarded, so the fit has
\code{chains * (iter - warmup)} posterior draws. Before using the results, check
\verb{$diagnostics}: \code{Rhat} compares the chains and should be below 1.01 for
every coefficient, and the effective sample sizes \code{ess_bulk} (for the
centre of the posterior) and \code{ess_tail} (for interval limits) should be at
least 100 per chain. If they are not, increase \code{iter}.
}

\section{Missing states}{

Use \code{NA} in the response for a visit whose state is unknown, keeping the row
(for example from \code{\link[=prepare_markov_data]{prepare_markov_data()}}, which keeps rows with a missing
response). \code{missing_states} says what a missing response means:
\itemize{
\item \code{"any"}: nothing is known about the visit, so the patient may also have
died (or entered another absorbing state) at or before it. Choose this
when vital status was not recorded at visits with a missing state.
\item \code{"non_absorbing"}: the patient was known to be alive, in some
non-absorbing state. Choose this when vital status is known at every
visit, so a missing state never hides a death. A missing visit then still
carries the information that the patient survived to it.
}

Without \code{absorbing_state} (the default \code{NULL}), no state is absorbing:
every state can be left, as on a quality-of-life scale that does not
include death. A missing state may then be any state under either rule, so
both rules give the same fit. If the data contain a state that is never
left, such as death, give it in \code{absorbing_state}. Otherwise the model
allows leaving that state, and a missing visit could imply, for example,
that a patient died and later recovered.

The chain follows the rows of each patient in \code{time_var} order. A visit
without a row is treated as not scheduled, not as a missing state, so keep
one row for every scheduled visit until an observed absorbing state or the
end of follow-up. In particular, do not end a patient's rows at a missing
state: if a death may have gone unrecorded, the later visits (showing the
death, or missing) must stay in the data, or the death is lost.

Rows after a missing state usually have a missing or wrong \code{p_var}. Only
the first row's \code{p_var} (the starting state, which must be observed) is
used; afterwards the previous state is taken from the response. Where the
previous row's state is observed, \code{p_var} must agree with it.
}

\section{States known up to a range}{

The response can be \code{\link[rms:Ocens]{rms::Ocens()}}\verb{(lower, upper)} instead of a single
state column, as in \code{\link[rms:orm]{rms::orm()}}: \code{Ocens(y_lo, y_hi) ~ rcs(time, 4) + tx + yprev}. With states 1 to 8 and death as state 8, \code{Ocens(1, 7)} means
"alive, level unknown" and \code{Ocens(b, b)} is an observed state \code{b}. Two
finite endpoints include both endpoints. A lower endpoint of \code{-Inf} means
"below the upper endpoint", and an upper endpoint of \code{Inf} means "above the
lower endpoint". Both endpoints \code{NA} is a missing state and follows
\code{missing_states}. Rows with observed, range, and missing states can be
mixed freely. Whether a state is reported exactly may not depend on the
unreported exact state.
}

\section{Assumptions}{

The fit is valid when the states are \emph{missing at random}: whether a state
is missing may depend on what was observed (for example earlier states or
treatment), but not on the unobserved state itself. If, say, patients in
worse health are more likely to miss a visit than observed patients in the
same previous state, the estimates are biased. Sensitivity analyses for
such missingness are outside the scope of this function.

The model must be first order: apart from \code{p_var}, every covariate must be
fixed per patient (such as treatment) or a function of time only. A
covariate computed from the state history, such as a lagged state or days
spent in hospital so far, would be wrong after a missing state and is not
allowed. The starting state and all covariates must be observed.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
data <- prepare_markov_data(trajectories_with_missing, absorbing_state = 6)
fit <- blrm_markov_ml(
  y ~ rms::rcs(time, 4) + tx + yprev,
  ppo = ~time,
  cppo = function(y) as.numeric(y),
  data = data,
  id_var = "id",
  absorbing_state = 6,
  missing_states = "any",
  cores = 4
)
fit$diagnostics
avg_sops(fit, variables = list(tx = c(0, 1)), times = 1:28)

# A random intercept per patient
fit_re <- blrm_markov_ml(
  y ~ rms::rcs(time, 4) + tx + yprev + cluster(id),
  data = data,
  id_var = "id",
  absorbing_state = 6,
  cores = 4
)
avg_comparisons(
  fit_re,
  variables = list(tx = c(0, 1)),
  times = 1:28,
  state_sets = list(death = "6"),
  include_re = TRUE
)
}

}
